####
# ======================================================================
# desktop_input_lock - Run a command holding the desktop input lock
# ======================================================================
#
# SYNOPSIS
#     desktop_input_lock hold [--speak] [--] COMMAND [ARGS...]
#     desktop_input_lock --help
#     desktop_input_lock --version
#
# DESCRIPTION
#     Run COMMAND while holding the lock that tests taking the keyboard
#     and mouse share, so it never lands its clicks and keystrokes in
#     another program's windows, and another program's never land in
#     its own.
#
#     A shell script has no flock(1) on macOS, and a lock taken by one
#     project's tests means nothing to another's unless both take the
#     same file the same way. This command takes the one every project
#     agrees on, waits for it if another process holds it, runs
#     COMMAND, and lets go when COMMAND ends.
#
#     It is silent by default. With --speak, the person at a Mac is
#     told by banner and aloud to step away, and called back once no
#     test has needed the keyboard for three seconds.
#
# COMMANDS
#     hold               Run COMMAND holding the lock.
#
# OPTIONS
#     --speak            Ask the person at a Mac to step away, and call
#                        them back afterward
#     --                 End the options; what follows is COMMAND
#     -h, --help         Show this help
#     -V, --version      Print the version
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#     DESKTOP_INPUT_LOCK   A lock of this session's own, in place of
#                          /tmp/desktop-input.lock
#     DESKTOP_INPUT_QUIET  Never speak, even with --speak
#     CI                   Never speak, even with --speak
#
# FILES
#     /tmp/desktop-input.lock  The lock every project shares
#     /tmp/desktop-input.away  Says the person was asked to step away
#
# EXIT STATUS
#     COMMAND's own      When COMMAND runs
#     128 + N            When signal N ends COMMAND
#     126                When COMMAND cannot be run
#     127                When COMMAND is not found
#     1                  Bad arguments
#
#     INT, TERM, and HUP sent to this command are passed on to COMMAND.
#
# EXAMPLES
#     Run a script that drives the iOS Simulator:
#     desktop_input_lock hold -- bin/drive-simulator --device iPhone
#
#     Run a long check that should ask the person to step away:
#     desktop_input_lock hold --speak -- bundle exec rake test:gui
#
# ======================================================================
###
# DesktopInputLock is requested: an AI agent built it, with little or no
# review from a person. Its gates and its real-process tests review it
# instead.

require "desktop_input_lock/cli"
exit DesktopInputLock::CLI.new(help_path: __FILE__).call(ARGV)
